From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 08 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 002] fix: log2.h fls() conflicts with the BSD fls() on Darwin

log2.h defines a static inline fls(unsigned int). Darwin declares fls(int)
in <strings.h> as a BSD extension, so the static definition follows a
non-static declaration of the same name and the compiler rejects it. glibc
has no fls() at all, which is why Linux never sees this.

The BSD function has the same semantics: it returns the one-based position
of the most significant set bit, and 0 for an argument of 0. Verified
against the bundled implementation over a dense sweep of the low million
values, every one and two bit pattern, and the sign boundary cases where an
unsigned argument converts to a negative int. No differences.

Keep the bundled version everywhere else and use the libc one on Darwin.
This header exists to serve hash.h, which is the only consumer in tree.
---
diff --git a/include/osmocom/core/log2.h b/include/osmocom/core/log2.h
index 8c65768f..c775df89 100644
--- a/include/osmocom/core/log2.h
+++ b/include/osmocom/core/log2.h
@@ -18,6 +18,14 @@
  * This is defined the same way as ffs.
  * Note fls(0) = 0, fls(1) = 1, fls(0x80000000) = 32.
  */
+/* Darwin declares the BSD extension int fls(int) in <strings.h>. Defining a
+ * static one here makes the compiler reject the static declaration that
+ * follows a non-static one. glibc has no fls(), so Linux is unaffected. The
+ * BSD fls() has the same semantics: it returns the 1 based position of the
+ * most significant set bit, and 0 for an argument of 0. */
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+#include <strings.h>
+#else
 static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) int fls(unsigned int x)
 {
 	int r = 32;
@@ -46,6 +54,7 @@ static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) int fls(unsigned int x)
 	}
 	return r;
 }
+#endif /* !__APPLE__ */
 
 /**
  * fls64 - find last set bit in a 64-bit word
